an active volcano off the coast of north  africa is getting more dangerous this  morning reports from la palma and  spain's canary island say one side of  the volcano has just collapsed spain's  prime minister has pledged nearly 240  million dollars in aid and insists that  the island is still safe for tourists  dan johnson from our partners at the bbc  is on the palma right now  incredibly this eruption is now into a  third week and it keeps getting stronger  the volcano is producing even more lava  with even more force that's why all that  lava all that ash is pouring up into the  sky higher and higher and that means  more of a risk to a bigger area  the potential for more people to be  evacuated on top of the 6 000 or so who  have been out of their homes for a  fortnight now and i was talking to the  director of the canary islands volcano  institute who said he expects this  eruption to continue for at least  another 10 days potentially another two  months and even then when the volcanic  eruption stops when the lava stops  flowing that's not the end of the story  he said it could take years to recover  from this because there are vast lava  trails right across the landscape here  there are people still living here right  in the shadow of the volcano some of  them saying they've had enough now after  a fortnight of that thing thundering  rumbling right through their lives day  and night some have had enough they  can't get sleep they want to leave  someone making the decision to get to  safer places but then i've also spoken  to people who live with that volcano  effectively in their back garden who say  no as long as the authorities will let  me i'm going to stay i'm going to see it  out the unanswered question still is  when that eruption will actually cease  when the lava will stop flowing and when  the destruction will finally stop for  cbs mornings this is dan johnson bbc  news la palma
 

